<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.12.1: Using the Slope Formula</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00252</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.12.1: Using the Slope Formula</md:title>
    <md:uuid>03aaccdc-6fab-4937-a276-223af3c845d3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (5 minutes)</title>
<para>The purpose of this warm up is to help students recall finding the slope of a line. Remind students that slope is the ratio of the change in \( y \) and the change in \( x \). Substituting is an important skill in writing equations. </para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Arrange students in groups of 2. Encourage students to write out each step and to use parentheses, especially when working with negative numbers. </para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Use the slope formula, \( m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \), to find the slope of the line through the given points.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\( (1, -2) \) and \( (3, 4) \)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> </para>
<para>1. \( m= 3 \)</para>
<para>First, name \( (1, −2) \) as point 1 and \( (3, 4) \) as point 2.
  \( (x_1, y_1) \) and \( (x_2, y_2) \)
  \( (1,−2) \) and \( (3, 4) \)</para>
<para>Use the slope formula.</para>
<para>\( m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \)
  Substitute the values: \( y_2 = 4, y_1 = -2, x_2 = 3, x_1 = 1 \).
  \( m=\frac{4-(-2)}{3-(1)} \)</para>
<para>Simplify.</para>
<para>\( m=\frac{6}{2} \)
  \( m= 3 \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\( (-2, -3) \) and \( (-7, 4) \)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> </para>
<para>\( m= -\frac{7}{5} \)
First, name \( (  −2, −3) \) as point 1 and \( (−7, 4) \) as point 2.
\( (x_1, y_1) \) and \( (x_2, y_2) \)  
\( (  −2,−3)\) and \( (−7, 4) \)</para>
<para>Use the slope formula.</para>
<para>\( m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \)
Substitute the values: \( y_2 = 4, y_1 =   −3, x_2 = −7, x_1 = −2 \).
\( m= \frac{4-(-3)}{-7-(-2)} \)  </para>
<para>Simplify.</para>
<para>\( m= \frac{7}{-5} \)
\( m= -\frac{7}{5} \)  </para>
<title>Student Response</title>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>Some students may be confused by the subscripts. Point out that the notation helps make sure that you are matching the correct \( x \)-value with the corresponding \( y \)-value. Demonstrate that you can name either point as \( (x_1, y_1) \) by having students find the slope by naming \( (-7, 4) \) as \( (x_1, y_1) \) and \( (-2,-3) \) as \( (x_2, y_2) \). </para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Ask students to share their methods for finding the slope. </para>
<para>Discuss with students:</para>
<list>
  <item>“How do you decide which point to use for \( (x_1, y_1) \) and \( (x_2, y_2) \)?” (Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. I substituted the first coordinate pair as \((x_1,y_1)\).)</item>
  <item>“Does it matter which point you choose for \( (x_1, y_1) \) and \( (x_2, y_2) \)?” (No.)</item>
  <item>“How can you make sure you use the correct corresponding \( x \) for each \( y \)?” (Your answer may vary, but here is a sample: Always start with the \(y\) in the second point, so start with \(x\) in the second point).</item>
</list>

</section>
